125
00:09:56.601 --> 00:09:59.840
Exercise 1 B takes the same
engine to sea level static

126
00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:06.262
conditions, at 450 kilograms per
second, so the core gets 64.29.

127
00:10:06.312 --> 00:10:09.578
Without ram compression but
with warmer air, the compressor

128
00:10:09.618 --> 00:10:15.346
exit is hotter, 909 kelvin, and
the overall pressure is 30.4 bar.

129
00:10:15.396 --> 00:10:19.899
All spool powers scale up with the
mass flow, about three times, and the

130
00:10:19.939 --> 00:10:26.346
fuel ratio is slightly lower, 0.0267,
because the burner entry is hotter.

131
00:10:26.396 --> 00:10:30.945
At sea level the ambient pressure is
high, so both nozzles turn out matched,

132
00:10:30.985 --> 00:10:38.159
the core at Mach 0.71 and the fan at Mach
0.58, a good reminder to always check.

133
00:10:38.209 --> 00:10:44.510
The take-off thrust is 104.3
kilonewtons with a T S F C of 16.44

134
00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:48.973
milligrams per second per newton,
both matching the official solution.

135
00:10:49.023 --> 00:10:53.273
And since the aircraft is not moving,
no useful power is produced: the

136
00:10:53.313 --> 00:10:57.625
propulsive and overall efficiencies
are zero by definition, even though

137
00:10:57.665 --> 00:11:03.653
the thrust is large; only the thermal
efficiency, 18.1 %, is meaningful.

138
00:11:05.303 --> 00:11:08.951
Two architectures from the
manual deserve a short mention.

139
00:11:09.001 --> 00:11:11.621
In a mixed-exhaust turbofan,
the core and bypass

140
00:11:11.661 --> 00:11:14.558
streams join in a mixer and
leave through one nozzle,

141
00:11:14.598 --> 00:11:18.163
the standard layout of military
engines with afterburners.

142
00:11:18.213 --> 00:11:21.478
Because the mixer requires
matching total pressures, the

143
00:11:21.518 --> 00:11:25.019
turbine pressure ratio is fixed
first, and the power balance

144
00:11:25.059 --> 00:11:28.561
then delivers the bypass ratio:
alpha becomes an output, the

145
00:11:28.601 --> 00:11:32.611
single structural difference from
the separate-exhaust engine.

146
00:11:32.661 --> 00:11:35.847
Mixing recovers thrust by
removing the velocity mismatch

147
00:11:35.887 --> 00:11:39.131
between the streams, a few per
cent, but on a large civil

148
00:11:39.171 --> 00:11:43.785
engine the full-length duct costs more
in weight and drag than it returns.

149
00:11:43.835 --> 00:11:47.294
The turboshaft splits the turbine:
the high pressure turbine

150
00:11:47.334 --> 00:11:50.735
drives the compressor, and a
free turbine drives the output

151
00:11:50.775 --> 00:11:54.060
shaft, judged by its power
specific fuel consumption; its

152
00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:58.883
propulsive efficiency only makes sense
together with its rotor or propeller.

153
00:12:00.533 --> 00:12:03.754
A propeller produces thrust
by giving a small velocity

154
00:12:03.794 --> 00:12:06.774
increase to a large mass
of air, the opposite of a

155
00:12:06.814 --> 00:12:11.030
turbojet, and momentum theory
captures it with an actuator disc.

156
00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:14.169
Bernoulli on each side of
the disc and the momentum

157
00:12:14.209 --> 00:12:17.666
balance give the central result:
the velocity at the disc

158
00:12:17.706 --> 00:12:21.798
is the average of the upstream
and far-downstream velocities.

159
00:12:21.848 --> 00:12:27.247
With the axial induction factor a, the
disc velocity is v zero times one plus a,

160
00:12:27.287 --> 00:12:32.821
the wake velocity v zero times one plus
two a, and the thrust two m dot a v zero.

161
00:12:32.871 --> 00:12:36.271
The disc power, thrust times
disc velocity, equals the

162
00:12:36.311 --> 00:12:39.711
kinetic energy given to the
slipstream, and the ideal,

163
00:12:39.751 --> 00:12:43.414
Froude, propulsive efficiency
is one over one plus a.

164
00:12:43.464 --> 00:12:46.712
Solving the quadratic for
the disc velocity shows that

165
00:12:46.752 --> 00:12:49.939
the loss is governed by the
disc loading, thrust over

166
00:12:49.979 --> 00:12:53.410
disc area: a big diameter means
high efficiency, but also

167
00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:57.514
high tip speeds, ground clearance
problems and root stresses.

168
00:12:59.164 --> 00:13:03.558
A real propeller absorbs more shaft
power than the ideal disc for the

169
00:13:03.598 --> 00:13:08.185
same thrust, because of profile drag,
swirl and tip losses, collected in

170
00:13:08.225 --> 00:13:13.330
a loss efficiency eta L, so the propeller
efficiency is eta L times eta P.

171
00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:17.438
For cycle work the course uses the
algebraic model plotted here: ten

172
00:13:17.478 --> 00:13:21.295
M zero eta max at very low speed,
constant at eta max up to Mach

173
00:13:21.335 --> 00:13:26.687
0.7, and falling beyond, when the
blade tips approach drag divergence.

174
00:13:26.737 --> 00:13:31.125
The procedure is: thrust from the
shaft power, disc velocity from the

175
00:13:31.165 --> 00:13:35.553
disc area, then the induction factor,
the disc power and eta L, which

176
00:13:35.593 --> 00:13:41.077
must not exceed one; otherwise the disc
is too small for the assumed efficiency.

177
00:13:41.127 --> 00:13:45.114
At zero speed the efficiency is
meaningless, so the static thrust

178
00:13:45.154 --> 00:13:49.266
uses the figure of merit, F equals
two rho A to the one third times

179
00:13:49.306 --> 00:13:54.966
F M times the power to the two
thirds, with F M around 0.7 to 0.8,

180
00:13:55.006 --> 00:13:58.994
while the model's own limit, ten
eta max times the power over the

181
00:13:59.034 --> 00:14:03.907
speed of sound, can violate momentum
theory, as exercise 1 B will show.

182
00:14:05.557 --> 00:14:07.590
A turboprop is a turboshaft whose free

183
00:14:07.630 --> 00:14:10.435
turbine drives a propeller
through a gearbox.

184
00:14:10.485 --> 00:14:14.748
It handles two streams, the gas
generator flow and the propeller flow,

185
00:14:14.788 --> 00:14:19.174
and their ratio beta behaves like a
bypass ratio, but it is an output of

186
00:14:19.214 --> 00:14:23.989
momentum theory, not a design choice,
because the propeller has no duct.

187
00:14:24.039 --> 00:14:28.089
The power reaching the propeller is the
gearbox efficiency times the shaft's

188
00:14:28.129 --> 00:14:32.783
share of the low pressure turbine power
minus the low pressure compressor power.

189
00:14:32.833 --> 00:14:37.105
The total thrust adds the jet and the
propeller, and the kinetic energy

190
00:14:37.145 --> 00:14:41.477
increment uses the disc power, so
propeller losses appear in the thermal

191
00:14:41.517 --> 00:14:45.970
efficiency; Mattingly uses the shaft
power instead, which gives a higher

192
00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:50.854
thermal and a lower propulsive efficiency
but the same overall efficiency.

193
00:14:50.904 --> 00:14:54.122
Finally, there is an optimum
free-turbine expansion: more

194
00:14:54.162 --> 00:14:57.837
expansion sends power to the
efficient propeller until the jet is

195
00:14:57.877 --> 00:15:02.457
starved, and the optimum shifts towards
the jet as the flight speed grows.

196
